Shepr change log. Newest first.
Every outside contribution is listed with the contributor's GitHub profile and, when one could be verified, their X profile.

2026-11-15  server only  the host serves
  `server.py PORT --tailnet`: Shepr serves its tailnet address itself, with nothing in front. It listens on the node's own tailnet
      addresses, tied to the Tailscale interface (a packet that arrives over the LAN finds nothing), speaks TLS with the certificate
      `tailscale cert` hands out (fetched and kept fresh by the server), and asks `tailscale whois` whose device is connecting before
      it reads a byte: the owner on a device with no tag, or the connection is closed with nothing sent. A phone keeps its TLS
      connection between requests, and only after a request that was read to its last byte. The server waits for Tailscale by itself
      (a boot where Tailscale is slow no longer fails the unit) and binds again when the interface is made anew.
      `tailscale serve` is no longer part of the install. A machine already behind it keeps working and moves over with one drop-in,
      keeping its address and its paired phones (README, "Already running behind `tailscale serve`?").
      An answer is now one write with Nagle off: a request over a kept connection took 110 ms from a second machine and takes 35.
      A refusal that goes out before an upload was read is followed by taking in the rest of it, so the phone reads the refusal
      and not a reset (HTTP/2 through `tailscale serve` used to hide that). A kept connection is asked about again at every
      request. `shepr status` says when a `tailscale serve` entry for the port was left behind, and when the certificate has run out.
      `tests/tailnet_test.py` (195 checks, real sockets, a stand-in `tailscale`) and `tests/hostile_lan.sh` (a namespace that is not
      on the tailnet must be refused) are new; the browser suites take `SHEPR_TEST_BASE` to run over a real listener.

2026-11-14  server only  Bendangelo  https://github.com/bendangelo
  X: https://x.com/bendangelo (linked from their own site bendangelo.me and the name matches, checked 2026-11-14)
  #5  With Tailscale turned off the unit cannot start (`tailscale serve` fails), and `shepr pair` answered "the server is not running,
      start it", which only starts the same wait again. When the server is down, `shepr pair` and `shepr status` now check Tailscale and
      say why ("Tailscale is turned off on this machine", "logged out", "tailscaled is not running", "still connecting") with the fix.
      The bar widget gets the same words in `shepr status --json` (`tailscale`). A running server is never asked: the widget polls it.
      `shepr pair` also no longer prints the server's own "tailnet requests will be refused" line, which it never needed.

2026-11-14  server only  shyam32 (skovuri41)  https://github.com/skovuri41
  X: none found (checked 2026-11-14: GitHub lists no X account and no social accounts, and there is no site to read)
  #4  Agents in named herdr sessions are shown. Every running session (`herdr --session work`) on the machine is a host of its own,
      called `gus/work`, so its agents reach the queue, the "blocked for" ledger, push and prepare/commit, and `shepr status` (the bar
      badge) counts them. The two sessions' pane ids stay apart. A session that stops drops out. `SHEPR_SESSIONS` limits which ones
      (as suggested), and a Shepr already started on a named session's socket (the old workaround) does not list it twice.

2026-10-14  server only  HANCORE  https://github.com/HANCORE-linux
  X: https://x.com/hancore_linux (listed on their GitHub profile, checked 2026-10-13)
  Second review of the Omarchy plugin (omacom/omarchy-plugin-marketplace#11393): the server read a POST body before it
  checked who was sending it, and its 20 second socket timeout restarted on every receive, so a caller trickling one
  byte at a time could hold all 48 connection slots for days. Fixed: the request line and headers have 5 seconds in all,
  a JSON body 10 and a photo 120, enforced from a timer that closes the socket; a caller who is not a paired phone is
  refused before any body is read; at most 16 bodies are read unsigned at once.

2026-10-12  v42 (1b97a6d)  HANCORE  https://github.com/HANCORE-linux
  X: https://x.com/hancore_linux (listed on their GitHub profile, checked 2026-10-13)
  Review of the Omarchy plugin for the marketplace (omacom/omarchy-plugin-marketplace#11176): the one-time pairing code
  was passed to qrencode as an argument, where `ps` could show it, and the QR picture could fall back to /tmp with default
  permissions. Fixed in 1b97a6d: the code goes over stdin, the picture is born 0600 in a private directory the CLI checks
  first, never /tmp, and Cancel removes it. v43 also removes it when `shepr pair` finishes waiting.

2026-10-10  v38  Jordan Partridge  https://github.com/jordanpartridge
  X: none found (checked 2026-10-10: GitHub lists no X account, only LinkedIn, and his site links none)
  #1  A session herdr no longer has is left, not polled. herdr answers agent_not_found, Shepr used to turn that into HTTP 400,
      and the phone asked again every 6 seconds. It now gets a 404, closes the session back to the queue and says "That session is gone."
  #2  `shepr pair` names the systemd unit installed on this machine. It reads ~/.config/systemd/user and names the unit that runs
      this server.py (symlinks included); with none installed it names both install styles instead of ordering one.
  #3  The install block in the README links `shepr` into whichever of ~/.local/bin or ~/bin your PATH already searches first,
      and falls back to ~/.local/bin, so `shepr pair` is found on the first try.
